"(PDF) Elasticity measurement of DNA origami nanotube in liquid with tapping mode AFM (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313888866_Elasticity_measurement_of_DNA_origami_nanotube_in_liquid_with_tapping_mode_AFM)\nciteturn84view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313888866_Elasticity_measurement_of_DNA_origami_nanotube_in_liquid_with_tapping_mode_AFM\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1315\nL0: * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Nanotechnology】\nL2:   * 【2†Nanoarchitecture】\nL3:   * 【3†Chemistry】\nL4:   * 【4†DNA Origami】\nL5: \nL6: Conference Paper PDF Available\nL7: # Elasticity measurement of DNA origami nanotube in liquid with tapping mode AFM\nL8: \nL9:   * April 2014\nL10: \nL11: DOI:【5†10.1109/NEMS.2014.7031669†doi.org】\nL12: \nL13:   * Conference: 2014 9th IEEE International Conference on Nano/Micro Engineered and Molecular Systems (NEMS)\nL14: \nL15: Authors:\nL16: \nL17: 【6† 】\nL18: \nL19: 【6†Longhai li】\nL20: \nL21:   * 【7†Chinese Academy of Sciences】\nL22: \nL23: 【8† 】\nL24: \nL25: 【8†Lianqing Liu】\nL26: \nL27:   * 【7†Chinese Academy of Sciences】\nL28: \nL29: 【9† 】\nL30: \nL31: 【9†Osamu Tabata】\nL32: \nL33:   * Kyoto University of Advanced Science\nL34: \nL35: 【10† 】\nL36: \nL37: 【10†Wen J Li】\nL38:   * 【11†City University of Hong Kong】\nL39: \nL40: 【117†Image†www.researchgate.net】\nL41: \nL42: 【12†Download full-text PDF】【13†Read full-text】\nL43: \nL44: 【14†Download full-text PDF】\nL45: \nL46: 【13†Read full-text】\nL47: \nL48: 【15†Download citation】\nL49: \nL50: Copy link Link copied\nL51: \nL52: * * *\nL53: \nL54: 【13† Read full-text】【15† Download citation】\nL55: \nL56: Copy link Link copied\nL57: \nL58: 【16†Citations (7)】【17†References (15)】【18†Figures (2)】\nL59: ## Figures\nL60: \nL61: 【19† 】\nL62: \nL63: 【19†Assumed modulus-Calculated modulus curve of PS nano-particle … 】\nL64: \nL65: 【20† 】\nL66: \nL67: 【20†Assumed modulus-Calculated modulus curve of DON … 】\nL68: \nL69: Figures - uploaded by 【10†Wen J Li】\nL70: \nL71: Author content\nL72: \nL73: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Wen J Li\nL74: \nL75: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL76: \nL77: 【118†Image: ResearchGate Logo】\nL78: \nL79: Discover the world's research\nL80: \nL81:   * 25+ million members\nL82:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL83:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL84: \nL85: 【21†Join for free】\nL86: \nL87: Public Full-text 1\nL88: Content uploaded by 【10†Wen J Li】\nL89: \nL90: Author content\nL91: \nL92: All content in this area was uploaded by Wen J Li on Jul 08, 2017\nL93: \nL94: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL95: \nL96: Elasticity measurement of DNA origami nanotube in\nL97: \nL98: liquid with tapping mode AFM\nL99: \nL100: Longhai Li 1,2, Lianqing Liu 1, Osamu Tabata 3,4,#, and Wen J. Li 1,5,*\nL101: \nL102: 1 State Key Laboratory of Robotics, Shenyang Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shenyang, China\nL103: \nL104: 2 University of Chinese Aca demy of Sciences, Beijing, China\nL105: 3 Department of Micro Engineering, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Japa n\nL106: \nL107: 4 Freiburg Institute for Advanced Studies (FRIAS), University of Freiburg, Freiburg, Germany\nL108: \nL109: 5 Deparment of Mechanical and Bi omedical Engineering, City Univer sity of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China\nL110: #For DNA self-assembly, contact: tabata@me.kyoto-u.ac.jp.\nL111: \nL112: *For Programmable AFM nanomani pulation, contact: wenjli@cityu.e du.hk.\nL113: \nL114: Abstract- The elasticity of nanomaterial is extremely difficult to be\nL115: \nL116: obtained  with traditional  method  due  to  their  ultra-small  scale.\nL117: \nL118: AFM  provide  a  feasible  way  to  solve  this  problem,  but  it  still\nL119: \nL120: meets  many  challenges  especially  for  the  measurement  of  one\nL121: \nL122: dimensional  material  such  as  DNA  origami  nanotube,  CNT,\nL123: Silicon Nanowire and  so  on.  In  addition  to  t he  influence  arouse d\nL124: \nL125: from the sample surface effect, locating the probe exactly over the\nL126: \nL127: 1D  material  with  nano-diameter  is  hard  to  achieve  due  to  the\nL128: \nL129: nonlinearity of PZT actuator  and the  thermal drift. In  this stu dy,\nL130: \nL131: a new method is proposed to  overcome these  shortcomings. It  is a\nL132: \nL133: physical  calculation  process  combined  with  experimental\nL134: \nL135: measurement  results  to  deduce  the elasticity  of  one  dimensional\nL136: material.  This  method  starts  at  an  assumed  elasticity  of  nano-\nL137: \nL138: materials, and  then experimental elasticity  can be obtained  bas ed\nL139: \nL140: on scanned image in tapping mode.  The elasticity with minimized\nL141: \nL142: error  between  assumed one  and experimental  one  should be  the\nL143: \nL144: true value  of  the materials.  Since  with the imaging scan  metho d,\nL145: \nL146: the exactly locating the probe over the sample is not necessary. In\nL147: \nL148: addition,  due  to  accurately  controllable  tapping  force,  the\nL149: deformation  of  the  nanomaterial can  be controlled  within  a tiny\nL150: \nL151: scale, thus the influence from the  sample surface effect can  be get\nL152: \nL153: rid  of  effectively.  The  elasticity  (pre-known)  of  polystyrene  i s\nL154: \nL155: measured  to  demonstrate  the  effectiveness  of  the  proposed\nL156: \nL157: method. The  elasticity  of  DNA  origami  is  also  first  time obtain ed\nL158: \nL159: with  the  proposed  method,  which  shows  an  elasticity  range\nL160: \nL161: between 75MPa and  180  MPa.  This  method  is  simple  and  can  be\nL162: used to measure other soft nano-materials.\nL163: \nL164: Keywords: DNA origami, DNA ori gami nanotube, Young’s modulus,\nL165: \nL166: AFM.\nL167: \nL168: I. I NTRODUCTION\nL169: \nL170: Since  the  first  2D  four-arm  branched  junction  assembled\nL171: \nL172: using single-stranded Deoxyribonucleic Acid chains (ssDNAs)\nL173: \nL174: by  Seeman  in 1981  [1], the  technique  of DNA  self-assembly\nL175: \nL176: has  been  developed  rapidly  due  to  its  great  potential\nL177: \nL178: applications. Many 2D/3D  DNA  nanostructures  were coming\nL179: to be available; especially, DNA origami [2], invented in 2006,\nL180: \nL181: which possesses potentially higher yields and simple operation\nL182: \nL183: compared with traditional DNA self-assembly technique. With\nL184: \nL185: the  precise  addressability,  metallic  and/or  semiconducting\nL186: \nL187: materials  are  easily  to  attach  at  desi gned  position  on  DNA\nL188: \nL189: origami  template  by  staple  strands,  or  DNA  building  blocks\nL190: \nL191: can  connect  with  each  other,  to  form nano/micro-device.  The\nL192: proposed  concept  was  named  programmable oriented self-\nL193: \nL194: assembly on MEMS (P-OSAM) [3], through which,  high-order\nL195: \nL196: device/system  of  micrometer-scale  can  be  constructed  by\nL197: \nL198: different  building  blocks  by  sticky  ends  of  ssDNAs.  For\nL199: \nL200: stabilization  of  system/device,  the  understanding  of  physical\nL201: \nL202: properties of DNA molecules or DNA origami building blocks,\nL203: \nL204: especially  elastic  property  (m echanical  property),  is  of  great\nL205: \nL206: significance.\nL207: Currently,  many  methods  were  developed  to  measure  t he\nL208: \nL209: mechanical  properties  of  1D  nanomaterials,  especially  DNA\nL210: \nL211: molecules. Bustamantet [4] et al. measured the Young’s\nL212: \nL213: modulus of λ-DNA  by  force-measuring  optical  tweezers,  in\nL214: \nL215: which  t he  λ-DNA  molecule  was  stretched  by  fixing\nL216: \nL217: microscopic latex bead attached on one end of chain on pipette\nL218: \nL219: and another bead attached on another chain end. The Young’s\nL220: \nL221: modulus of λ-DNA calculated by measuring the extension and\nL222: the  pulling  force  is  about  346  MPa.  Croquette  [5]  utilized\nL223: \nL224: magnet  bead  instead of  latex  bead to  stretch  the single  DNA\nL225: \nL226: molecule.  Although  these  methods  have  been  demonstrated\nL227: \nL228: effectively for the  Young’s  Modulus  measurement  of  long\nL229: \nL230: DNA  chains,  they  show  poor  efficiency  for  short  chains  or\nL231: \nL232: nano-structures.  Atomic  force  microscope  (AFM),  as  one  of\nL233: \nL234: the most popular tool  used  for  nanotechnology,  has  been  used\nL235: for measuring Young’s modulus of nanomaterials in  decades.\nL236: \nL237: Yoon’s group [6] fixed one end of DNA chain on the S i surface\nL238: \nL239: while stuck another end on AFM tip, by lifting up tip off the S i\nL240: \nL241: surface, the force-distance c urve was measured to calculate the\nL242: \nL243: elastic property of DNA. And th e Young’s modulus of ssDNA\nL244: \nL245: and  double  stranded  DNA  (dsDNA)  were  measured  to  be\nL246: \nL247: 978-1-4799-4726-3/14/$31.00 © 2014 IEEE 684\nL248: \nL249: Proceedings of the 9th IEEE International\nL250: Conference on Nano/Micro Engineered and Molecular Systems\nL251: \nL252: April 13-16, 2014, Hawaii, USA\nL253: \nL254: 18±0.5 MPa and 55±3.2 MPa, respectively. The problem here\nL255: \nL256: is  that  the number  of  chains that  attached on  the  AFM tip is\nL257: \nL258: unclear. In addition, all the methods mentioned above can only\nL259: \nL260: get the stretching elastic property in axis direction, not the one\nL261: \nL262: in radius  direction.  In 1995, Hu  et  al. [7]  developed  vibrating\nL263: \nL264: scanning polarization force microscopy to scan λ-DNA\nL265: molecules, and found that the  radial elastic property of λ-DNA\nL266: \nL267: is not constant, but was 20-70 MPa under 0.4 nN tapping force,\nL268: \nL269: and  increased  to  100-200  MPa  with  improved  tappi ng  force.\nL270: \nL271: The shortcoming of this method is that it doesn’t work in liquid\nL272: \nL273: environment,  the  materials  existe d  in  liquid  (such  as  cell,\nL274: \nL275: DONs) can not  be  measured.  Pang  et al. [8] supposed that the\nL276: \nL277: bending energy of  vibrated  cantilever  is  transferred  into  DNA\nL278: perfectly  and  leads  to  a  com pression,  based  on  which  they\nL279: \nL280: obtained the Young’s modulus of DNA to be 100-300 MPa. In\nL281: \nL282: fact,  the  assumption  may  be  not  true,  because  the  bending\nL283: \nL284: energy of cantilever is  converted into different energy, such a s\nL285: \nL286: thermal energy, energy for plas tic deformation and so on.\nL287: \nL288: In  this  paper,  we  propose  a  new  method  to  measure  the\nL289: \nL290: Young’s modulus of 1D nanomaterials by tapping mode AFM,\nL291: named assumption-calculation method (ACM). This  method is\nL292: \nL293: a  physical  calculation  process  combined  with  experimental\nL294: \nL295: measurement  results  to  deduce  the  elasticity  of  one\nL296: \nL297: dimensional material. It starts at an assumed elasticity of nan o-\nL298: \nL299: materials,  and  then  experimental  elasticity  can  be  obtained\nL300: \nL301: based  on  scanned image  in  t apping mode.  The  elasticity with\nL302: \nL303: minimized  error  between  assumed  one and  experimental one\nL304: should  be  the true  value of  the materials.  The elasticity  (pre-\nL305: \nL306: known)  of  polystyrene  nanoparticles  (PS)  is  measured  to\nL307: \nL308: demonstrate  the  effectiveness  of  the  proposed  method.  The\nL309: \nL310: elasticity of  DNA  origam i is  also  first time  obtained  with the\nL311: \nL312: proposed  method,  which  shows  an  elasticity  range  between\nL313: \nL314: 75MPa and 180 MPa.  This  method  is  simple  and  can be used\nL315: \nL316: to measure other soft nano-materials.\nL317: \nL318: Fig. 1. Structure of DNA origami nanotube\nL319: II. M ATERIALS AND M ETHOD\nL320: \nL321: A. Materials\nL322: \nL323: DNA  origami  nanotubes (DONs),  with  diameter 6  nm  and\nL324: \nL325: length 400 nm (as shown in Fig. 1), has been prepared with the\nL326: \nL327: method  developed  by Kiss  et al.  [9].  The DONs,  which were\nL328: \nL329: made from a long single  strand DNA and more than 200 short\nL330: \nL331: single strand DNAs through annealing two hours from 94 ćto\nL332: \nL333: 20 ć, were used to demonstrate the ACM. The DONs solution\nL334: \nL335: was  deposited  onto  mica  surface  that  was  treated with  20 ul\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Thermal stability of DNA origami on mica (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262604812_Thermal_stability_of_DNA_origami_on_mica)\nciteturn84view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262604812_Thermal_stability_of_DNA_origami_on_mica\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1229\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: \nL2: # Thermal stability of DNA origami on mica\nL3: \nL4:   * May 2014\nL5:   * 【0†Journal of Vacuum Science and Technology B: Nanotechnology and Microelectronics】 32(4):040602-040602-4\nL6: \nL7: DOI:【1†10.1116/1.4879417†doi.org】\nL8: \nL9: Authors:\nL10: \nL11: 【2†Michelle A. Pillers】\nL12: \nL13:   * 【3†Saint Louis University】\nL14: \nL15: 【4†Marya Lieberman】\nL16: \nL17:   * 【5†University of Notre Dame】\nL18: \nL19: 【6†Image†i1.rgstatic.net】\nL20: \nL21: 【7†Download full-text PDF】【8†Read full-text】\nL22: ## Abstract and Figures\nL23: \nL24: The authors report the unusual thermal stability of deoxyribose nucleic acid (DNA) origami when adhered to a solid substrate. Even when heated to 150 °C for 45 min, these DNA nanostructures retain their physical and chemical integrity. This result suggests that DNA origami could be integrated into applications requiring moderate substrate heating, such as photoresist baking or chemical vapor deposition processes.\nL25: 【9†(Color online) AFM images and height measurements of DNA origami on mica (note: all images are taken at the same scale and in air). (a) Untreated DNA origami nanostructures exhibit a height of approximately 2 nm; individual measured heights may appear less than 2 nm due to DNA origami collapse upon drying. (b) Upon heating to 150 C for 10 min, there is minimal structural or height change of the DNA origami.\nL26: (C) After 10 min at 250 C, there is visible structural degradation and the height of the DNA origami decreases by half to approximately 1 nm.… 】\nL27: Figures - uploaded by 【4†Marya Lieberman】\nL28: \nL29: Author content\nL30: \nL31: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Marya Lieberman\nL32: \nL33: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL34: \nL35: 【10†Image: ResearchGate Logo】\nL36: \nL37: Discover the world's research\nL38: \nL39:   * 25+ million members\nL40:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL41:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL42: 【11†Join for free】\nL43: \nL44: Public Full-text 1\nL45: \nL46: Content uploaded by 【4†Marya Lieberman】\nL47: \nL48: Author content\nL49: \nL50: All content in this area was uploaded by Marya Lieberman on Sep 23, 2015\nL51: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL52: \nL53: Thermal stability of DNA origami on mica\nL54: \nL55: Michelle A. Pillers and Marya Lieberman\nL56: \nL57: Citation: Journal of Vacuum Science & Technology B 32, 040602 (2014); doi: 10.1116/1.4879417\nL58: \nL59: View online: http://dx.doi.org/10.1116/1.4879417\nL60: \nL61: View Table of Contents: http://scitation.aip.org/content/avs/journal/jvstb/32/4?ver=pdfcov\nL62: \nL63: Published by the AVS: Science & Technology of Materials, Interfaces, and Processing\nL64: \nL65: Articles you may be interested in\nL66: Thermal treatment effects imposed on solid DNA cationic lipid complex with hexadecyltrimethylammonium\nL67: \nL68: chloride, observed by variable angle spectroscopic ellipsometry\nL69: \nL70: J. Appl. Phys. 116, 234701 (2014); 10.1063/1.4904196\nL71: \nL72: Thermomechanical stability and mechanochemical response of DNA: A minimal mesoscale model\nL73: \nL74: J. Chem. Phys. 141, 115101 (2014); 10.1063/1.4895724\nL75: \nL76: Cooperativity in the annealing of DNA origamis\nL77: \nL78: J. Chem. Phys. 138, 015105 (2013); 10.1063/1.4773405\nL79: Selective placement of DNA origami on substrates patterned by nanoimprint lithography\nL80: \nL81: J. Vac. Sci. Technol. B 29, 06F205 (2011); 10.1116/1.3646900\nL82: \nL83: Structure and thermal stability of gold nanoplates\nL84: \nL85: Appl. Phys. Lett. 88, 071904 (2006); 10.1063/1.2175500\nL86: \nL87: Redistribution subject to AVS license or copyright; see http://scitation.aip.org/termsconditions. Download to IP: 129.74.250.206 On: Mon, 14 Sep 2015 20:42:11\nL88: \nL89: Thermal stability of DNA origami on mica\nL90: \nL91: Michelle A. Pillers and Marya Lieberman\nL92: \nL93: a)\nL94: Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, University of Notre Dame, 271 Stepan Chemistry Hall,\nL95: \nL96: Notre Dame, Indiana 46556\nL97: \nL98: (Received 14 April 2014; accepted 12 May 2014; published 22 May 2014)\nL99: \nL100: The authors report the unusual thermal stability of deoxyribose nucleic acid (DNA) origami when\nL101: \nL102: adhered to a solid substrate. Even when heated to 150 C for 45 min, these DNA nanostructures\nL103: \nL104: retain their physical and chemical integrity. This result suggests that DNA origami could be\nL105: integrated into applications requiring moderate substrate heating, such as photoresist baking or\nL106: \nL107: chemical vapor deposition processes. V\nL108: \nL109: C 2014 American Vacuum Society.\nL110: \nL111: [http://dx.doi.org/10.1116/1.4879417]\nL112: \nL113: I. INTRODUCTION\nL114: \nL115: Integration of the large scale deterministic patterning of\nL116: \nL117: lithography with the sub-10 nm patterning capabilities of de-\nL118: \nL119: oxyribose nucleic acid (DNA) origami would advance the\nL120: \nL121: fabrication of electronic, photonic, and magnetic nanosys-\nL122: tems. However, many lithographic processes involve heating\nL123: \nL124: a substrate to elevated temperatures, which might damage\nL125: \nL126: the DNA nanostructures. For example, deposition of SiO\nL127: \nL128: 2\nL129: \nL130: via plasma enhanced chemical vapor deposition (PECVD) is\nL131: \nL132: carried out at temperatures between 150 C and 250 C; pre-\nL133: \nL134: and postbaking of photoresist-covered wafers is usually done\nL135: \nL136: on a hotplate at 100–150 C; and metal deposition often\nL137: \nL138: requires postdeposition thermal annealing at temperatures as\nL139: \nL140: high as 600 C.\nL141: \nL142: 1,2\nL143: Since short strands of duplex DNA typi-\nL144: \nL145: cally melt at temperatures below 80 C, thermal stability of\nL146: \nL147: DNA origami is a concern.\nL148: \nL149: 3\nL150: \nL151: The melting temperature of helical segments of DNA in a\nL152: \nL153: buffer solution can be predicted based on the number of base\nL154: \nL155: pairs, the G:C ratio, the concentrations of monovalent and\nL156: \nL157: divalent cations, and the buffer p H; normal duplex DNA of\nL158: \nL159: any length, and G:C ratio is denatured at temperatures above\nL160: \nL161: 90 C.\nL162: \nL163: 4\nL164: \nL165: DNA nanostructures such as tile arrays and origami\nL166: consist of normal duplex DNA with a high density of crossover\nL167: \nL168: structures that interweave neighboring helices.\nL169: \nL170: 5\nL171: \nL172: However,\nL173: \nL174: these interconnections do not provide special thermal stability.\nL175: \nL176: DNA origami in buffer solutions begin to lose their structures\nL177: \nL178: at about 50 C and are completely denatured at temperatures\nL179: \nL180: above 70 C.\nL181: \nL182: 6,7\nL183: \nL184: Even with photo-cross-linked DNA origami\nL185: \nL186: designed for increased thermal stability, complete denatura-\nL187: \nL188: tion of the origami structure is observed in solution at 90 C.\nL189: \nL190: 8\nL191: Because solution denaturation of normal origami is reversible\nL192: \nL193: in the presence of excess staple strands, thermal denaturation\nL194: \nL195: followed by annealing is the most common method for assem-\nL196: \nL197: bly of DNA origami and other nanostructures.\nL198: \nL199: 9–11\nL200: \nL201: Thermal\nL202: \nL203: annealing in solution can even be applied to grow well-formed\nL204: \nL205: tile arrays on mica substrates.\nL206: \nL207: 12,13\nL208: \nL209: When DNA origami are placed on a solid substrate in air,\nL210: \nL211: there are no water molecules to help stabilize partially disso-\nL212: ciated segments of DNA, and ionic interactions between so-\nL213: \nL214: dium or magnesium ions and backbone phosphate groups or\nL215: \nL216: anions on the substrate are greatly strengthened.\nL217: \nL218: 14\nL219: \nL220: These\nL221: \nL222: ionic interactions are critical for anchoring DNA strands and\nL223: \nL224: nanostructures to anionic surfaces.\nL225: \nL226: 15,16\nL227: \nL228: We chose to study\nL229: \nL230: the thermal stability of DNA origami on mica, which is the\nL231: \nL232: most common substrate for DNA origami analysis. Using\nL233: \nL234: atomic force microscopy (AFM) to detect structural changes\nL235: and x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) to detect chemi-\nL236: \nL237: cal changes, we investigated the thermal stability of DNA\nL238: \nL239: origami on mica.\nL240: \nL241: 17,18\nL242: \nL243: Our results show that DNA origami\nL244: \nL245: are compatible with the lower end of processing tempera-\nL246: \nL247: tures used in common lithography operations, and therefore\nL248: \nL249: might ﬁnd wider utility as resists or patterning aids for high-\nL250: \nL251: resolution surface patterning.\nL252: \nL253: II. EXPERIMENT\nL254: \nL255: A. Materials\nL256: \nL257: The rectangular DNA origami was based on a previously\nL258: \nL259: reported design.\nL260: \nL261: 19\nL262: The template M13 viral strands were pur-\nL263: \nL264: chased from Bayou Biolabs (Metairie, LA), and the DNA sta-\nL265: \nL266: ple strands were synthesized by Integrated DNA Technologies\nL267: \nL268: (Coralville, IA). Mica was purchased from electron microscopy\nL269: \nL270: sciences (Hatﬁeld, PA). AFM tips (NSG30 series) were pur-\nL271: \nL272: chased from K-Tek Nanotechnology LLC (Wilsonville, OR).\nL273: \nL274: B. Assembly of DNA origami\nL275: \nL276: Staple strands were pooled to make a staple strand mix.\nL277: \nL278: The design required 4 l L of 197 different staple strands and\nL279: 212 l Lof18M X water to achieve a total volume of\nL280: \nL281: 1000 l L. The staple strands were then mixed with M 13 viral\nL282: \nL283: DNA, TAE/Mg\nL284: \nL285: 2 þ\nL286: \nL287: buffer, and 18 M X water to give a total\nL288: \nL289: volume of 200 l L. The mixture contained a 1:10 ratio of\nL290: \nL291: M13 viral DNA to DNA staples and a 1:10 ratio of the\nL292: \nL293: TAE/Mg\nL294: \nL295: 2 þ\nL296: \nL297: buffer to total solution. A 2 nM solution of DNA\nL298: \nL299: origami was prepared using1 l L of M 13 viral DNA, 10 l L\nL300: \nL301: of staple strand mixture, 20 l L of TAE/Mg\nL302: \nL303: 2 þ\nL304: \nL305: buffer, and\nL306: 169 l Lof18M X water. Annealing was completed by heat-\nL307: \nL308: ing the solution to 90 C for 5 min and then cooling to 20 C\nL309: \nL310: over the span of 70 min. The DNA origami was not ﬁltered\nL311: \nL312: or puriﬁed. Annealed DNA origami was stored at 4 C.\nL313: \nL314: C. Surface preparation and AFM imaging\nL315: \nL316: Mica sheets were cut into 1 cm 1 cm squares. The top\nL317: \nL318: layer of the mica substrate was exfoliated using double sided\nL319: \nL320: a)\nL321: \nL322: Electronic mail: mlieberm@nd.edu\nL323: 040602-1 J. Vac. Sci. Technol. B 32(4), Jul/Aug 2014 2166-2746/2014/32(4)/040602/4/$30.00 V\nL324: \nL325: C 2014 American Vacuum Society 040602-1\nL326: \nL327: Redistribution subject to AVS license or copyright; see http://scitation.aip.org/termsconditions. Download to IP: 129.74.250.206 On: Mon, 14 Sep 2015 20:42:11\nL328: \nL329: tape to remove the top-most mica layer. DNA origami was\nL330: \nL331: deposited on the mica in a 5 l L drop for 10 min at room tem-\nL332: \nL333: perature. The mica was rinsed with sterile water and dried\nL334: \nL335: with N\nL336: \nL337: 2\nL338: . A programmable hotplate (Cole-Parmer 04644\nL339: \nL340: Series Programmable Digital Hot Plate Magnetic Stirrer)\nL341: \nL342: was used to heat the samples at the required temperatures.\nL343: \nL344: Images were acquired in air in tapping mode using a Digital\nL345: \nL346: Instruments Nanoscope V Scanning Probe Microscope.\nL347: \nL348: AFM images were analyzed using NanoScope Analysis soft-\nL349: \nL350: ware (Bruker).\nL351: \nL352: D. XPS imaging\nL353: \nL354: XPS spectra were acquired using a PHI VersaProbe II.\nL355: \nL356: Survey spectra used a 100u25W15 kV x-ray setting with a\nL357: pass energy of 187.50 eV over ten cycles. For more high re-\nL358: \nL359: solution elemental scans, the pass energy was changed to\nL360: \nL361: 23.500 eV over one cycle of between 10 and 30 sweeps per\nL362: \nL363: selected element. Electron neutralization was used with an\nL364: \nL365: emission current of 20 l A, a bias voltage between 1.0 and\nL366: \nL367: 1.6 V, extractor lens voltage of 30 V, and ﬁlament current of\nL368: \nL369: between 1.05 and 1.20 A. Ion gun neutralization using Ar\nL370: \nL371: þ\nL372: \nL373: was used with a 0.500 kV beam, target emission of 15 mA,\n"
